{
  "result": "I have always loved the hour just before the city wakes completely. The streets are quiet but not empty, and the air feels newly washed. A baker lifts his shutters and warm bread breathes into the cold morning. Somewhere a bus sighs at the curb and carries sleepy people toward ordinary miracles. I like walking then because my thoughts seem louder and kinder than usual. Problems that felt enormous at midnight shrink to a size I can hold. I think about the people I have been and the person I am still becoming. Some versions of me were brave in ways I only understand now. Others were foolish, tender, impatient, and certain they already knew everything worth knowing. All of them left something useful in my pockets for the road ahead. Sometimes I pass the river and watch light arrive in slow gold pieces. A heron stands there like a patient question that does not need answering. I envy that stillness and I borrow a little of it for myself. By the time shops open and phones begin their bright demanding chorus, I feel steadier. The day will bring noise, errands, weather, news, and small unexpected kindnesses from strangers. It will also bring mistakes, because every honest day seems to include a few. But morning teaches me that beginning again is not failure; it is practice. So I turn for home, grateful for cold air, warm bread, quiet streets, and time. That is enough to start again with hope today.",
